54:36.941 --> 54:44.386
[SPEAKER_00]: product. And we have enough time, so please... go ahead as detailed as you want to, because this is so exciting, man. I think audiences want to

54:44.426 --> 54:54.393
[SPEAKER_00]: know about this. OK, so origami arises from an idea of going to harnesses that provide more performance. And to provide more performance, as

54:54.493 --> 55:03.161
[SPEAKER_01]: I've explained before, the idea is to reduce wake strength downstream the pilot, but also another approach is to reduce the cross-section

55:03.281 --> 55:12.887
[SPEAKER_01]: area. So if you fly with your legs straight, it's better in terms of aerodynamics than flying with your legs tilted. So, following these

55:12.927 --> 55:22.353
[SPEAKER_01]: two approaches for Drifter 2, what we did is to optimize the aerodynamic shape using simulations and aerodynamic shape optimizations. And the

55:22.393 --> 55:31.598
[SPEAKER_01]: other approach was to try to reduce this cross-section area as much as possible. Of course, you have several restrictions there because the

55:31.638 --> 55:41.514
[SPEAKER_01]: pilot is sitting in a fixed position. You cannot reduce the volume that the pilot assumes. Also you have to install the rescues if you install

55:41.534 --> 55:49.921
[SPEAKER_01]: them on the back and there's the protector. And of course the unique option there to reduce this cross-section area was to reduce the

55:50.222 --> 55:59.669
[SPEAKER_01]: thickness of the protector. So that was our motivation and taking this idea into account to end up with a Drifter 2 that is a really performant

55:59.689 --> 56:08.416
[SPEAKER_01]: harness that is comparable to full inflated harness in terms of performance. We decided to go to this really thin protector that

56:08.476 --> 56:16.662
[SPEAKER_01]: provides the same or even higher level of protection compared to conventional foams, but the thickness can be reduced more than 60%

56:17.183 --> 56:27.530
[SPEAKER_01]: compared to the foams that are used nowadays for these cross-country harnesses. and so that was our motivation so we put a lot of efforts from

56:27.630 --> 56:36.416
[SPEAKER_01]: our research and development department to try to investigate a solution that was really efficient in terms of protective behavior.

56:36.936 --> 56:48.002
[SPEAKER_01]: Efficient in the sense that it provides the same level of protection but with really lean profile. So with this idea in mind, what we did was to

56:48.102 --> 56:55.185
[SPEAKER_01]: look for the state of the art and the conventional technologies that are used nowadays, especially in the aerospace industry. There are

56:55.285 --> 57:03.588
[SPEAKER_01]: several different solutions, especially for helicopters, energy absorbers. If there's a crash in a helicopter, they are using honeycomb

57:03.668 --> 57:14.551
[SPEAKER_01]: structures made up of aluminum. So the idea was to research... You mean for the whole cage of the aircraft, the honeycomb is like basically the

57:14.571 --> 57:23.033
[SPEAKER_00]: skeleton of the chopper? Well, for aircraft they use it for the wings or so on, but in helicopters they use it as an energy absorbing device. So if

57:23.213 --> 57:32.355
[SPEAKER_01]: the helicopter, the engine fails and the helicopter starts to fall, they have these devices to absorb the energy from the impact and protect

57:33.035 --> 57:40.558
[SPEAKER_01]: the pilot and the occupants inside the cabin. So there's an application on this kind of observers in the aerospace industry and what we did was to

57:40.598 --> 57:49.302
[SPEAKER_01]: look for the state of the art and find or make a combination of the existing solutions and end up with this product, which is Oricami. That

57:49.402 --> 57:58.305
[SPEAKER_01]: is, I would say, really innovative product for the public lighting industry. And that's why we decided to make a patent application. So we

57:58.385 --> 58:06.827
[SPEAKER_01]: will, it will be approved soon and we will release all the information, technical aspects and so on because it's patented. And well, with these

58:06.927 --> 58:16.378
[SPEAKER_01]: different technologies that exist, we research for both the shapes and all the parameters that guide the design of origami and also the

58:16.398 --> 58:28.741
[SPEAKER_01]: materials that it is made of. Okay, so with all these combination of parameters and materials, what we achieve is a really effective and

58:28.781 --> 58:40.132
[SPEAKER_01]: efficient protective device or a protector because different physical phenomena The first one is the energy absorbing mechanism

58:40.172 --> 58:48.103
[SPEAKER_01]: that we use. We rely on our energy absorbing mechanism is elastic buckling deformation rather than plastic deformation. So what it

58:48.343 --> 58:57.234
[SPEAKER_01]: gives us is that it is suitable for several successive impacts without being damaged. Wow. That is revolutionary because earlier, I think any

58:57.295 --> 59:04.883
[SPEAKER_00]: protector, even the helmets, it's like if they take one impact, then they're discarded for use. But this one, you're saying that it can take

59:04.963 --> 59:14.889
[SPEAKER_00]: hits and still maintain the safety aspect of it. Exactly. And I can prove it because we follow the conventional homologation procedure that is

59:15.169 --> 59:22.976
[SPEAKER_01]: followed for each single piece of paragliding equipment that is sold in the European Union. There are two different certification

59:23.016 --> 59:32.705
[SPEAKER_01]: procedures. The difference between them is that EN, you only need to drop test the protector once. In LTF, you need to drop test the protector

59:32.805 --> 59:40.333
[SPEAKER_01]: twice. So you drop test it once. you take the same protector and drop test it again. And if the two values are within the homologation range, you

59:40.353 --> 59:48.822
[SPEAKER_01]: can approve this harness with this protector. In our case, what we did is to follow the homologation procedure and a straightforward process.

59:48.862 --> 59:59.210
[SPEAKER_01]: So we started with the same protector for EN, we drop tested it, Once we take it, we drop tested it again because you need to switch in between the

59:59.430 --> 01:00:08.317
[SPEAKER_01]: rescue parachute installed and without. And we did the same for LTF, always with the same protector. So at least we followed six high energy

01:00:08.417 --> 01:00:17.445
[SPEAKER_01]: drop tests and all these six consecutive drop tests show that the results were within the range of the homologation. so that's why we can

01:00:17.966 --> 01:00:26.954
[SPEAKER_01]: prove that orikami give protection for at least six high energy impacts but of course since safety is our main concern and it should be the same

01:00:26.994 --> 01:00:35.603
[SPEAKER_01]: for the pilots we recommend all the pilots to check the protector if they have a crash just to be sure that there's any broken part or so on but of

01:00:35.663 --> 01:00:45.563
[SPEAKER_01]: course during the emulation we prove that it sustains at least six high energy impacts You know, this product is kind of so interesting because

01:00:45.583 --> 01:00:54.126
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm forgetting the name of the other kind of... Choriode, yes. Choriode Protector. What is Choriode Protector? Because from what I

01:00:54.186 --> 01:01:04.550
[SPEAKER_00]: understand, it hardens when it faces stress. Otherwise, it's kind of a bit of a jelly-like structure. But of course, we have seen some crashes

01:01:04.590 --> 01:01:14.514
