01:01:14.674 --> 01:01:25.182
[SPEAKER_00]: how can a protector like that go out after the certification, out to the end product, and actually cause undesirable circumstances in the use

01:01:25.222 --> 01:01:34.871
[SPEAKER_00]: case scenario. What is Corihood and what went wrong with Corihood? I really want to know that. Well, I prefer not to talk about the concurrent

01:01:34.931 --> 01:01:42.398
[SPEAKER_01]: products, but I can explain to you why this is homologated and it actually is not protected in different scenarios that are not covered

01:01:42.418 --> 01:01:50.563
[SPEAKER_01]: by the homologation. It's because in demologation you only drop test it in a vertical direction, and if the direction is not correctly

01:01:50.643 --> 01:01:59.688
[SPEAKER_01]: vertical, the species can rotate or not be correctly fixed inside the slots of the phone. In our case, and it's another important aspect, is

01:01:59.728 --> 01:02:08.553
[SPEAKER_01]: that we did this drop test in different orientations and inclinations of the harness just to be sure that the pieces of origami state correctly

01:02:08.593 --> 01:02:17.118
[SPEAKER_01]: fix it inside the four matrix and we prove that the values of the maximum g's and also the time that in between all these two ranges of the

01:02:17.138 --> 01:02:24.703
[SPEAKER_01]: homologation are always the same depend and are independent of this orientation during the fall so that's an important aspect from our side

01:02:24.823 --> 01:02:34.512
[SPEAKER_01]: and also We designed Orikami to stay securely fixed inside these foam slots because we increased the out-of-plane stiffness. So, for

01:02:34.552 --> 01:02:42.220
[SPEAKER_01]: instance, if you take the piece of Orikami and you press it from the sides, it's not deforming or it's almost not deforming. And that's the

01:02:42.240 --> 01:02:51.229
[SPEAKER_01]: reason that explains that it stays correctly fixed inside this slot inside the foam. Yeah. Now, what I'm wondering is when you say putting

01:02:51.369 --> 01:02:59.696
[SPEAKER_00]: pieces of origami under there, and of course, the other protectors are the foam one or the airbag or the one which kind of form the whole

01:02:59.856 --> 01:03:08.624
[SPEAKER_00]: structure of the harness. Having a whole structure of the protector of origami, is that too expensive? Is that too financially unviable? Why

01:03:08.644 --> 01:03:17.333
[SPEAKER_00]: do we have to only put pieces of it and then worry about them being twisted or turned depending upon the direction of impact? Well, I think that the

01:03:17.433 --> 01:03:25.779
[SPEAKER_01]: explanation is not economic. Of course, there's an economic point there because it would be more expensive. But the explanation is that

01:03:26.039 --> 01:03:38.907
[SPEAKER_01]: when you have a crash, normally the parts that crash are the pelvis and are the bones. Andrea, so with Oricami also we investigated the most

01:03:38.967 --> 01:03:48.050
[SPEAKER_01]: potential places where the impact starts. So we place Oricami wisely in that positions just to be sure that they are there to absorb the energy

01:03:48.110 --> 01:03:56.213
[SPEAKER_01]: just at the beginning of the crash and absorb the or maximize the absorbing capacity of this Oricami piece. So that explains also this

